source":"gtwo_kml_202611202310","idp_filedate":1795216258000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"A complex and large low pressure system includes three embedded low  pressure centers, one over the Gulf of America, another over  west-central Florida, and a third a few hundred miles east-northeast  of the northwestern Bahamas. All of these systems are currently  non-tropical. However, the system over the western Atlantic still  has some potential to acquire subtropical or tropical  characteristics over the next day or two while it moves northward,  passing near the North Carolina and mid-Atlantic coasts on Sunday.  1. Although the chances of subtropical or tropical development appear  to be decreasing, this large weather system is still expected to  produce gusty winds, dangerous surf, rip currents and coastal  flooding along portions of the southeast and mid-Atlantic U.S.  coasts into early next week. In addition, heavy rainfall and the  threat of flash flooding will continue across portions of the  northeast Florida peninsula tonight, spreading northward over  coastal portions of the Carolinas and the mid-Atlantic over the  weekend. Interests along the eastern coast of the United States  should continue to monitor the progress of this system. Additional  information on this system, including Gale Warnings, can be found in  High Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather Service, as well  as products issued by your local National Weather Service forecast  office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...30 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...low...30 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}